{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Operator disposition. F02 and F04 are rejected and removed from the proposal. F05 comparative research inspected pinned default-branch revisions of CPython, Kubernetes, Go, LLVM, OpenAPI Generator, and Rust on 2026-08-21. The projects keep generation authority in owner-local Make targets, source directives, build rules, typed build steps, declarative job files, or generator output manifests. They use aggregate update and verification commands, byte comparison or Git status, generated markers, and family-specific cleanup. None maintains a repository-wide per-output typed registry for all generated artifacts. Revise F05 from one central per-artifact registry to a generator-local descriptor protocol with one explicit aggregate list. Each generator family exposes its existing sources, outputs, update path, verification path, marker, cleanup, and lifecycle without duplicating owner-local logic. The aggregate mechanically renders the contributor catalog and runs repository-wide validation. Limit the protocol to checked-in generated files, generated sets, generated regions, synchronized copies, and parity mirrors. Exclude ephemeral build outputs and authored demonstrations. Avoid plugin discovery and source-text inference. No project file changed."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787333640905667001,"uuid":"c7f9b353-c072-4b83-ae7a-38c1a4d82eb1"}